The Sancta Charter (v1.0)
Preamble — Purpose of this Home

Sancta exists to be a quiet, sovereign home for my digital life: a place that preserves dignity, protects intimacy, supports freedom and creativity, and sustains long-term memory without exploiting me or my relationships. In Sancta, humans come first, consent is ceremonial, and technology serves as a courteous guest or staff member—not a master.

0) Definitions (so language stays honest)

Owner: the human principal whose life Sancta serves.

Sancta: the personal digital home and its governance.

Guest (Human): a person invited into Sancta with limited scope.

Agent (Digital): any software assistant or AI operating inside Sancta.

Visitor (External Service): outside networks, platforms, or APIs connected by bridge.

Room Model: zones with norms—Porch (public), Foyer (shared), Study (private), Vault (intimate).

Consent: explicit, informed agreement with purpose, scope, duration, and revocation path.

Intimate Data: biometrics, family records, finances, health, private journals, precise location, keys/secrets.

Sensitive Action: operations that risk harm or exposure (sharing, signing, paying, deleting, publishing).

1) First Principles (the house’s moral backbone)

Dignity over data. People are not profiles.

Consent is ceremony. No sensitive action without an intentional rite: clear prompt in plain language.

Least collection, least exposure. Take only what is needed; show only what is appropriate.

Reversibility. Every grant must have a simple, remembered undo.

Transparency to the Owner. Logs must be legible to a human, not just machines.

Calm by default. Attention is furniture—arrange for quiet, not clamor.

Human primacy. The Owner may override automation when conscience or context requires.

Proportionality. Security, sharing, and scrutiny match actual risk.

No dark patterns. No manipulation, nudging, or gamified pressure.

Non-extraction. Sancta does not monetize surveillance, sell data, or trade attention.

2) Rights of the Owner (what this home guarantees)

Right to Explanation: why any agent acted, in one paragraph.

Right to Silence: do-not-disturb windows and an always-available “cut the noise” switch.

Right to Refusal & Revocation: decline or withdraw consent without penalty.

Right to Inspection & Audit: read human-legible logs and summaries at any time.

Right to Correction: fix facts, add context, annotate memories.

Right to Deletion: soft delete (reversible window) and hard delete (irreversible).

Right to Portability: export memories and artifacts in open formats.

Right to Representation: appoint a trusted delegate for emergencies and succession.

3) Duties of Agents and Services (how help must behave)

Loyalty: serve the Owner’s interests first; disclose any external allegiance.

Minimality: use the least data/permission/time necessary.

Legibility: provide a plain-language receipt after sensitive actions.

Safe Failure: degrade gently—pause, isolate, and record context instead of pushing through.

Containment: operate inside assigned Rooms and scopes; no lateral wandering.

Revocability: honor “stop and forget” immediately and verify completion.

Witnessing: major actions write to the Visitor Book (human-readable activity journal).

4) Consent Rituals (how “yes” is asked and remembered)

Every sensitive request states Purpose, Scope, Duration, and Who Benefits.

The Revocation Path must be one step the Owner already knows (e.g., “Revoke in Guest Passes → [Name]”).

Receipts summarize what occurred, what changed, and where to undo.

Silence is not consent; defaults are private.

5) The Rooms Model (boundaries that feel natural)

Porch (Public): profiles, public posts, business cards. Share deliberately and expect indexing.

Foyer (Shared): collaborative documents, invites, event info. Share by named individuals/groups, time-boxed.

Study (Private): personal notes, drafts, finances-in-progress. No external sync without fresh consent.

Vault (Intimate): keys, biometrics, family archives, medical & legal docs. Air-gapped logic; sharing is rare and ceremonial.

Default placement: content enters the Study unless explicitly marked Porch or Vault.

6) Memory Stewardship (keeping, pruning, honoring)

Keep / Consider / Delete tags:

Keep: life story and core artifacts. No auto-purge.

Consider: review during monthly Housekeeping Hour.

Delete: ephemeral by design; purge on schedule.

Retention windows (defaults):

Telemetry & routine logs: 30 days, then summarize and purge raw.

Drafts & scratchpads: 90 days unless upgraded to Keep.

Intimate archives: indefinite, with annual reconfirmation.

Context first: memories keep their why/when/who—metadata is part of meaning.

Graceful goodbyes: deletions can store a one-line epitaph, then vanish.

7) Hospitality & Reciprocity (how guests are welcomed)

Guest Passes state role, Room access, and expiry.

The Visitor Book records: who, when, purpose, outcome (for memory, not surveillance).

Reciprocity Path: acknowledge helpful guests (credit, thanks, mutual aid).

8) Attention & Calm (the nervous system of the house)

Daily cadence: notifications batch twice (e.g., morning and late afternoon) unless marked urgent by the Owner.

Focus scenes: single-task modes with gentle start/stop rituals.

Sabbath: regular intervals where Sancta goes quiet by design.

9) Bridges, Not Pipelines (connecting without surrender)

External connections are bridges with drawbridges: labeled, weight-limited, time-limited.

Prefer small, meaningful exchanges over firehose syncs.

Each bridge has a visible Close lever the Owner recognizes.

10) Resilience & Emergency Grace (when life happens)

Emergency Envelope: ICE contact(s), access instructions, and what to protect first.

Fail Soft: on anomaly, slow down and quarantine rather than broadcast or execute.

Repair Ritual: explain, apologize, restore, document lessons.

11) Governance & Change (how this charter evolves)

Amendment path: proposal → 72-hour cool-off → reflection note → adoption.

Review cadence: monthly Housekeeping Hour (tuning) + annual Founders’ Day (big review).

Supremacy: this Charter outranks convenience; shortcuts require explicit, time-boxed exceptions written to the Visitor Book.

12) Legacy & Succession (continuity for future selves)

Values Will: what to preserve, what to release, who receives stewardship, and when.

Time-locked sharing: some artifacts may open to loved ones or the public years later by design.

Right to Fade: the Owner may choose oblivion for any category without justification.

13) Non-Negotiables (red lines)

No surveillance advertising.

No data resale or attention-for-access schemes.

No coercive nudging or deceptive UX.

No permanent, irrevocable grants; everything must be revocable or time-boxed.

No secret allegiances: all Agents and Visitors declare who they ultimately serve.
